\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}
Prior work on random number generation falls into four strands that this systematization draws
on and connects.

\textbf{Standards and theory.} The foundations are set by standards and by the theory of robust
generators: RFC 4086, the NIST SP 800-90 series, the Yarrow design, and the robustness model of
Dodis \etal{} define what a sound generator is and how to build
one~\cite{rfc4086,nist80090a,nist80090b,nist80090c,kelsey1999yarrow,dodis2013devrandom}. We adopt
their decomposition for stages \stage{1} to \stage{5} and use them as the backbone of the remedy
catalog. Textbooks already stress that statistical tests cannot certify cryptographic
randomness~\cite{aumasson2024serious}. We add the deployed evidence for that limit.

\textbf{Population measurement.} A second strand measures a single failure class at Internet
scale: weak keys from low boot-time entropy, RSA moduli that share a prime, and reused ECDSA
nonces mined from public ledgers~\cite{heninger2012mining,lenstra2012ron,bos2014ecc}. These
quantify individual mechanisms with rigor. We generalize across mechanisms and add the remedy and
counterfactual dimensions.

\textbf{Single-incident analyses.} A third strand dissects individual incidents in depth: the
Debian defect, the \dualec{} and Juniper affair, ROCA, DUHK, the CTR\_DRBG cache attacks,
TPM-Fail, Minerva, and OpenSSL fork-safety~\cite{yilek2009debian,checkoway2016juniper,nemec2017roca,cohney2018duhk,cohney2020blackswan,moghimi2020tpmfail,jancar2020minerva,strenzke2016openssl}.
Our contribution is to place these on a common taxonomy and to ask, uniformly, which deployed
remedy would have prevented each.

\textbf{Construction surveys.} A fourth strand surveys generator constructions and their testing
within a domain, most comprehensively for the IoT~\cite{kietzmann2021prng}. Such surveys organize
algorithms and statistical batteries. They do not systematize deployed \emph{failures}, and, being
construction-centric, they do not model the build and integration surface (\stage{$\ast$}) that
produced the Debian and \coldcard{} incidents.

\textbf{Positioning.} To our knowledge no prior work places deployed randomness failures from
browsers, TLS, and SSH through smart cards, consoles, mobile platforms, virtualization, and trusted
platform modules to wallets on one stage model that includes consumption and the build surface,
and grades every incident against the same remedy catalog under stated rules. The hedged and derandomized cryptography
line gives the structural defenses we find most
effective~\cite{bellare2009hedged,rfc6979,bernstein2012ed25519,ristenpart2010vmreset,corrigangibbs2013ensuring}.
We situate those defenses within the broader remedy space and test them against the incident
record.
